WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.590
BRIAN LEVEE: Hi, I'm Brian
Levee, a product manager

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:09.760
on the Google Docs team.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.440
And this is Allen Yang, another
product manager on the team.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.200
Nowadays, people use a
number of tools at work

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.780
in order to get things done.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.700
Over the last few
years we've worked

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.630
to integrate Google Docs
into the G Suite applications

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.980
as well as third party tools.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.060
But in order to further
improve users' workflows,

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.510
we know that we need to
go further to seamlessly

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:37.090
integrate Google Docs
into the applications

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:38.155
that users use most.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.810
All right, I'll do it,
I'll do it by hand.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:02.540
Today, we're going to go over
existing integrations that

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.450
are offered by Doc
Sheets and Slides

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:09.200
as well as introduce three
new types of integrations that

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.420
are currently in development.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:15.590
We have Slides add-ons,
Templates integrations,

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.867
and Storage integrations.

00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:19.700
We've been working with
a number of partners

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.500
on these new
integrations, and I'm

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.120
excited to have
two of them with us

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.390
here today to walk us
through the integrations that

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:27.730
are currently being worked on.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.770
Let's start with the
existing integrations

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:37.170
offered by Google Docs,
add-ons in Docs and Sheets

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.000
and API for Sheets and Slides.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.910
Docs and Sheets add-ons
allow third party developers

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.340
to add functionality
that allow users

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.570
to accomplish new workflows
within the editors.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.870
Add-ons can be installed from
within Docs and Sheets using

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.380
the top level add-on
menu, and then

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.520
are installed from
within the editor

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.300
through the Chrome Web Store.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.490
Add-ons can interact with
Documents and Spreadsheets

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.870
in a number of manners including
showing sidebars and dialogues

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.640
within the editor.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.820
We've seen add-ons in Google
Docs that have performed

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.440
a number of use cases.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.280
There are add-ons that make
it easy to insert and create

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.000
diagrams directly in
your document, add-ons

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.410
that provide writing aids
to users to check grammar

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.400
and document readability.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.840
There are add-ons that
offer translation services,

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:39.610
as well as business tools
such as Document Signatures,

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.510
and Templates.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.360
Lucidchart is an example
of a popular add-on

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.330
in Google Docs that
makes it easy for users

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:53.370
to insert as well as to create
diagrams ranging from my mind

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:59.640
maps to flowcharts and
other types of diagrams,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.500
all from within the
Google Docs Editor.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.640
Sheets add-ons as
well has been created

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.590
for a variety of use cases.

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.630
We've seen Sheets
add-ons that allow

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.450
for cross-product
integrations, add-ons

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:16.690
that pull in data from
external sources such as Google

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.100
Analytics and third parties.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.090
There are add-ons that help
with advanced formatting

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.610
in order to make spreadsheets
look professional,

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.470
as well as add-ons
that provide workflow

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:31.800
assistance such as Automatic
Document Creation and Mail

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:32.300
Merge.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.410
Let's take a look at
yet another Mail Merge,

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.350
which is a popular Sheet's
Add-on, in this case created

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.290
by an independent developer.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.900
Yet another Mail Merge
makes it easy for users

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.760
to import their contacts
while in Google Sheets

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.759
and then send mail merges
without even having to leave

00:03:53.759 --> 00:03:54.800
the Google Sheets Editor.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:01.180
That was add-ons
for Docs and Sheets.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:06.250
Last year, we also introduced
APIs for Sheets and Slides.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.770
These APIs make it
easy for developers

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:11.740
to pull and push data
and content in and out

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:13.990
of spreadsheets
and presentations,

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:16.570
making it easy to automatically
generate spreadsheets

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:18.820
and presentations,
as well as keep them

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:21.850
in sync with third party tools.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:25.330
Whereas, you saw add-on
user flows typically

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:28.930
starting from within the
editor, API user journeys

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.770
often start from the
third party tool.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:36.820
We've seen the Sheets
API used for integrations

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.820
across a variety of
use cases including

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:43.570
syncing customer
data with your CRM,

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:47.650
making sure your timelines
stay in sync in the spreadsheet

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.840
with your project
management tool,

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.940
and exporting data
from the tools

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.100
that you use most
into Sheets to make

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.700
it easy to manipulate the data
and create visualizations.

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:03.530
Let's take a look at
an example integration

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.770
that Asana created
using the Sheets API.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:11.450
From within Asana, users
can view project dashboards

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.300
in order to see how
projects are going

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:18.740
and keep track of what's
going on on their teams.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:20.480
When you're managing
a number of teams

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.880
this quickly becomes
difficult to do.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.640
So Asana used the
Sheets API in order

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:30.140
to allow users to open these
reports in Google Sheets where

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:34.850
they can create visualizations
that make it easy for the team

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:38.000
leaders and managers to keep
track of what's going on

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.490
and identify what areas
need their attention.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.660
In addition, leveraging
the Sheets API,

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:49.490
Asana was able to keep the
data in Sheets up-to-date

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:51.410
with any changes
made back in Asana.

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:56.960
Sauna Similarly,
the Slides API has

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.630
been used for a
variety of use cases,

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.000
automatically
generating presentations

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.390
that pull in customer
data from CRMs,

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:08.450
adding project updates to
presentations from project

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:13.355
management tools, and exporting
diagrams, visualizations,

00:06:13.355 --> 00:06:14.105
and other content.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:21.370
ProsperWorks used the
Slides API in order

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:26.620
to allow users to tag templated
fields in Google Slides

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:29.590
with metadata that allows
ProsperWorks to pull

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:35.910
in associated customer
data directly from the CRM

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:39.200
into dynamically
created presentations.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.880
No more having to manually
copy-paste all of this customer

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:48.020
data from the CRM into each
new presentation that you make.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:50.960
ProsperWorks makes it easy
to automatically generate

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.060
these presentations
for you, saving you

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.760
a lot of time and
energy, and creating

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.900
dynamic presentations that
merge with your customer data.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:07.300
So we've covered add-ons
in Docs and Sheets, which

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.020
bring the power of third
party tools into the editors,

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:14.170
as well as APIs for
Sheets and Slides, which

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:16.930
allow spreadsheets and
presentations to stay

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:20.630
in sync with third party tools.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:24.230
We've gotten great feedback
from both our users,

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:27.560
our customers, and our partners
on these integrations, which

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.900
is why we're very
excited to announce

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:32.800
three new types of
integrations in Google Docs,

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.500
Sheets, and Slides that are
currently in development.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:40.580
We have Slides add-ons,
Templates Integrations,

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:42.480
and Storage Integrations.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.410
To walk us through
these new integrations,

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:48.170
we're going to
introduce Lois a sales

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:52.640
manager at a retail company in
the Midwest who's going to help

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:54.775
us walk through some examples.

00:07:54.775 --> 00:07:57.500
I'll now hand it over
to Allen to walk us

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:02.960
through add-ons in Slides
and Template Integrations.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:04.490
ALLEN YANG: Thanks, Brian.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.630
And thanks to all of you
for being here today.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:09.330
We're really excited to share
what we've been working on.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:14.300
So Lois is wanting to
make a presentation

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:17.990
to help pitch a new product
line to her senior leadership.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:19.940
In order for her slides
to be successful,

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:23.020
they're going to need to
be punchy and polished.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.910
As you know, Google
Slides is a great product

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:27.680
to help you make
presentations that

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:29.570
are beautiful and
collaboratively made

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:31.030
with your team.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:32.960
Today, we're very
excited to announce

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:36.320
that the same add-ons framework
that exists for Docs and Sheets

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:39.710
today is coming
to Slides as well.

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:42.860
Soon, users will be able to
use Slides add-ons to leverage

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:46.580
partners such as Balsamiq,
Lucidchart, and Adobe

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:49.160
Stock in order to insert
beautiful diagrams

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:52.570
and photos into their slides.

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:54.640
We made Slides add-ons
with the intent

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:57.730
to help users become more
efficient by eliminating

00:08:57.730 --> 00:08:59.950
unnecessary context
switches, and we'll

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:03.110
see what that means
in a little bit.

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:05.680
First, let's take a look at
what our partners at Adobe Stock

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.180
have come up with.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:10.840
Lois has drafted her
deck, but now she

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:13.240
wants to add a bunch
of high quality images

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.700
in order to help
emphasize her point.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.820
To do this, she
thinks there might be

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.570
a Slides add-on that can help.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:22.690
So she visits the top
level add-ons menu

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:26.720
and goes into the add-ons store.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:28.370
In the store, she
notices that there

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:32.090
is a new add-on from Adobe Stock
to help her with stock photos,

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:35.480
so that's the one she
chooses to install.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:38.270
Using this add-on,
Lois now has access

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:42.620
to Adobe Stock's entire database
of high quality stock photos.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:44.600
And using this
add-on sidebar, she

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:46.820
can search through those
photos by category,

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:53.540
such as icons, as well as search
by theme, such as workplace.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.430
For any particular
photo, she can

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:57.980
choose to buy the license
to use that photo,

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:00.980
she can preview it
in her slide, or she

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:04.800
can look at similar
images from Adobe Stock.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.670
When she is settled on the
photos she wants to use,

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:10.700
she can log into Adobe Stock
using either her Adobe Stock

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:14.450
credentials or using
her Google account.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:17.120
Previously, in order to
accomplish the same workflow,

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:20.079
Lois would have had to
open a new tab or window,

00:10:20.079 --> 00:10:21.620
browse through the
partner's database

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:23.960
there, purchase the
licenses to those images

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:27.290
there, and then import those
photos back into Slides.

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.540
But now, with this
new Slides add-on,

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:31.970
Lois is able to accomplish
the same workflow

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:34.100
all within the Slides interface.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.870
This is exactly what
we mean by saying,

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:40.790
we want to reduce
unnecessary context switches.

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:43.220
So moving on into
deck, Lois wants

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:45.590
to create a rather
complex process flow

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:48.710
diagram to help her
company troubleshoot

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:51.050
defective new designs.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:54.050
Normally, she could do this
by creating a bunch of shapes

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:56.870
and slides, filling
those shapes with text,

00:10:56.870 --> 00:11:00.140
connecting them with lines and
arrows, and then figuring out

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:03.320
how to lay those out on a
slide in an appealing way.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.680
But what if she had access to a
more powerful diagramming tool

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:08.210
like Lucidchart?

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:11.060
Well, we're excited to announce
that our partners at Lucidchart

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:13.790
are also working
on a Slides add-on.

00:11:13.790 --> 00:11:17.840
With this add-on, Lois can log
into her Lucidchart account

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:21.230
and see a list of all
her previous diagrams.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:23.060
Lois remembers that
at one point in time

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:25.460
she did create a
troubleshooting flow.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:28.920
And indeed, she finds it
here in her list of files.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:32.120
She is able to preview this
diagram in the lower right

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:34.390
corner of the add-on sidebar.

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:36.170
And by hitting the
blue insert button,

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:39.777
she can insert that diagram
as an image into her slide.

00:11:39.777 --> 00:11:41.360
After doing this,
though, she realizes

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:43.318
that she wants to make
a couple of small tweaks

00:11:43.318 --> 00:11:44.150
to this diagram.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:47.360
So she hits the Edit button
in the middle of the sidebar

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:51.200
and gets taken to
Lucidchart's powerful new web

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.390
diagram editor.

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:57.060
This allows Lois to use the full
power of the Lucidchart tool

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:00.200
to edit the diagram
in any way she wishes.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:03.320
When she's done, she
can return to her slide.

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:05.330
And by simply hitting
that refresh button

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:09.380
in the lower right hand corner,
the new version of the diagram

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:12.570
will replace the old version.

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:13.780
Great.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:18.700
So Lois now has successfully
completed her diagram slide,

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:22.000
but she also wants to create
a few wireframes of what

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.760
the web page for this new
product might look like.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:27.160
To do this, she can use
the Balsamiq add-on.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:30.040
Balsamiq is a great tool
that helps you quickly create

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:33.760
wireframes and prototypes.

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:36.610
Lois has installed
the Balsamiq add-on.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:39.550
And she notices that it connects
with her Google Drive, which

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:42.700
is where Balsamiq
stores all of her work.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:44.530
Using the add-on,
Lois can browse

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:46.900
through her entire list
of all Balsamiq projects

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:48.340
that she's worked on.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:50.200
And with the
sidebar she can even

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:52.450
search through those
different projects.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:54.490
When she double clicks
on a particular project

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:56.860
she can see the individual
wireframes or marks

00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:58.360
that make up that project.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.510
And she gets a preview
of each of those mocks.

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:04.150
For any given mock, she
can hit the Edit button

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:07.390
to be taken to Balsamiq's
powerful web editor, which

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:10.480
brings the full
experience of Balsamiq

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:14.230
to bear on any diagram
she wants to edit.

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:18.340
When she's done, she can go
back to slides and insert

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:22.000
the wireframe or
mock into her slide.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.670
Later on, she knows that
if she makes any edits

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:26.350
to a particular
mock or wireframe,

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:29.140
she can always refresh
all the mocks in her desk

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:32.630
to get the latest version.

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:34.880
These are just some
examples of Slides add-ons

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:36.560
that our partners
are working on.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:38.899
If there are any
developers in the room who

00:13:38.899 --> 00:13:40.940
would be interested in
working with us to develop

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:43.730
a Slides add-on, please
feel free to drop

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:45.770
us a note at the
URL on this slide

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:47.870
because we'll be opening
a Trusted Tester Program

00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:49.910
in just a couple of months.

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:52.250
If you're excited to
use Slides add-ons,

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:53.895
they'll be coming
out later this year

00:13:53.895 --> 00:13:56.270
and we're excited to have a
great set of launch partners.

00:13:56.270 --> 00:13:59.310
That will be in the
Chrome Web Store.

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:01.140
Using this new
integration, Lois is

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:03.540
able to create a
beautiful presentation,

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:06.390
successfully pitch her new
product line to leadership,

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:07.800
and get it approved.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:09.840
She then goes out to
a potential supplier

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:12.240
and negotiates a great
deal for her company,

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.600
but now she needs to
encapsulate that negotiation

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.840
into a formal document that
both parties will sign.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:22.660
To do this, our next Docs
integration will help.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:24.700
Template integrations.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:27.640
As you may know, towards
the end of last year

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:29.650
Google Docs released the
ability for companies

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:32.897
to have their own internal
company template galleries.

00:14:32.897 --> 00:14:35.230
And at this point, I'd love
to see a quick show of hands

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:37.120
for anybody who has
had a chance to try out

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:40.575
these new galleries in the Doc
Sheets or Slides home screens.

00:14:40.575 --> 00:14:42.650
Great, great,
decent number here.

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:43.150
Terrific.

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:45.340
Well, we hope you've
enjoyed that experience.

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:47.256
And if you haven't had
a chance to try it yet,

00:14:47.256 --> 00:14:50.000
we highly, highly
encourage you to do so.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:53.360
So template integrations are
the next step in Templates.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:56.650
A template integration is a
pairing of a specific template

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:58.990
with a specific add-on.

00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:02.050
With this pairing, we're excited
to bring the power and domain

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:05.020
expertise of our partners
to the flexibility of Google

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.290
Docs, Sheets, and Slides.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:09.040
This feature is coming
out later this year

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:10.600
and is currently
under development.

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.720
But at launch there will be a
set of Google curated template

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:17.320
integrations as well as the
ability for enterprise users

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:20.830
to pair their own templates with
any add-ons they have access to

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:23.770
and upload that pairing
to their company gallery.

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:27.630
Let's take a quick look
at how this helps Lois.

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:29.180
So Lois suspects
that her company

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:33.060
has a number of boilerplate
legal contract templates.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.760
So she goes to the
Docs home screen

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:38.240
and looks at her company's
internal template gallery.

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:40.640
Indeed, there she
finds that her company

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:43.400
has a standardized sales
order form, which she can

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:45.230
use for this particular case.

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:47.510
And she even notices that
there is an add-on attached

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:48.540
this template.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:51.470
So this is the one she selects.

00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:54.740
She gets taken to the
editor, seeing the template,

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.440
but she also notices that
this template has compared

00:15:57.440 --> 00:15:59.660
with the DocuSign add-on.

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:02.330
Turns out Lois's
company uses DocuSign

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:05.030
for all of its electronic
signature needs.

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:08.540
And one of Lois's coworkers has
helpfully created this template

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:10.880
and paired it with
the DocuSign add-on

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:13.310
to be uploaded to
the company gallery.

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:17.120
And this way, Lois knows
exactly the right tool to use.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:19.220
Through this new
sidebar, Lois is

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:22.370
able to kick off the insulation
flow for the DocuSign

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:25.520
add-on, which will then
request the permissions it

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:26.210
needs to run.

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:30.910
After it's finished
installing, Lois

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:34.670
is able to launch the DocuSign
add-on from the sidebar.

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:36.430
This means that Lois
had the right tool

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:39.700
ready at her fingertips
for the appropriate moment.

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:42.940
This is accomplishing our
goal of making workflows more

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:45.980
efficient for enterprise users.

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:48.340
So this DocuSign
add-on is actually

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:51.140
a new add-on that's
currently under development.

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:53.784
DocuSign is a recommended
for G Suite partner

00:16:53.784 --> 00:16:55.450
and already has a
number of integrations

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:57.360
with other parts of G Suite.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:00.440
Their new Docs add-on is
their latest integration

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:03.700
and so today we're very excited
to have Marshall Nam, Product

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:06.010
Manager at DocuSign, who
will walk us through what

00:17:06.010 --> 00:17:08.002
this experience looks like.

00:17:08.002 --> 00:17:08.710
Thanks, Marshall.

00:17:12.494 --> 00:17:15.020
MARSHALL NAM: Thank
you very much, Allen.

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:17.170
Quick show of hands, I'm
curious, how many of you

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:19.524
have heard of DocuSign before?

00:17:19.524 --> 00:17:21.190
OK, so I have a good,
friendly audience.

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:22.599
Thank you, this is good.

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:25.089
How many of you have actually
signed a DocuSign document

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:25.929
before?

00:17:25.929 --> 00:17:26.970
So about the same number.

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:29.650
I'm going to say
about 85% of you.

00:17:29.650 --> 00:17:32.950
And this is the real test, how
many people here have actually

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:36.280
used DocuSign to send
a document to somebody,

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:38.980
to get a signature
from somebody else?

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:42.806
So that number cut by about
2/3, only about a third of you

00:17:42.806 --> 00:17:43.430
have done that.

00:17:43.430 --> 00:17:44.400
And that's good.

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:44.900
That's good.

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:47.210
I think I have something
very cool to show you,

00:17:47.210 --> 00:17:50.005
you'll get to see the other side
of that signature transaction

00:17:50.005 --> 00:17:51.790
that you've been part of before.

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:54.165
And you'll also see how we're
using the power of Template

00:17:54.165 --> 00:17:56.260
integrations to really
unlock additional value,

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:59.050
make things a lot easier for
people that are using both G

00:17:59.050 --> 00:18:02.570
Suite and DocuSign together.

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:06.440
So Louis, Lois, now that she's
installed the DocuSign add-on,

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:09.287
using that very easy and
intuitive experience,

00:18:09.287 --> 00:18:11.120
first gets a summary
of the different things

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:14.300
are going to happen when
she goes through a DocuSign

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:15.570
transaction.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:18.279
So for this sales form,
she'll click start,

00:18:18.279 --> 00:18:19.820
and the first thing
she'll do, she'll

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:22.220
have that document automatically
imported into DocuSign,

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:24.440
and she'll see a quick
preview of it there.

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:26.690
But if she needs to add
additional things, maybe terms

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:29.150
and conditions, some
supplemental forms, things

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:30.777
she has to do for
her company, she

00:18:30.777 --> 00:18:32.360
can reach right back
into Google Drive

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:36.541
and pull those additional
documents in right there.

00:18:36.541 --> 00:18:38.540
And since we're integrated,
we can automatically

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:39.990
pull her information as well.

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:42.200
And we recognize that the
way that the template is

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:45.052
structured, since
it's add-on ready,

00:18:45.052 --> 00:18:47.010
we know that she's going
to be the first signer

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:48.080
on the document.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:50.339
And for Olivia, her
customer that she's

00:18:50.339 --> 00:18:52.130
signing this deal with,
all she has to have

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:53.780
is her name and
her email address.

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:57.130
And she fills that in, and it
continues to the next step.

00:18:57.130 --> 00:18:59.530
And now DocuSign will notify
her that she has something

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:01.532
available for your signature.

00:19:01.532 --> 00:19:03.490
And for those of you have
signed with DocuSign,

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:06.620
which was about 85% of you,
you've seen that email before.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:08.810
On this side, you can
actually customize that email

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:12.279
and send a message maybe
saying, hey Olivia, it's Lois.

00:19:12.279 --> 00:19:13.820
This is the document,
the sales order

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:15.320
form we agreed on,
please sign this.

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:19.775
For the third of you
that said that you've

00:19:19.775 --> 00:19:21.150
been through this
process before,

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:25.080
this is the most time consuming
part of DocuSign in the sending

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:25.581
experience.

00:19:25.581 --> 00:19:27.413
This is the part where
you take the document

00:19:27.413 --> 00:19:29.640
and you have to actually
mark on the document-- this

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:30.860
is where you're
going to sign, this

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:32.318
is where you have
to add your name,

00:19:32.318 --> 00:19:34.080
this is where the
date is going to go.

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:36.660
And through the power of
the template integrations

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:39.060
that we have now available
to us at G Suite,

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:40.840
we've done something
very interesting.

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:42.960
If you remember back to
the original document,

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:44.490
it looked like a
normal document,

00:19:44.490 --> 00:19:46.656
completely blank in all
those different fields where

00:19:46.656 --> 00:19:48.780
you had to sign and add data.

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:50.580
But because this
is DocuSign ready,

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:52.890
because it's integrated
with this template,

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:56.010
we have automatically recognized
all the different places

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:57.960
where Olivia has to
put her signature,

00:19:57.960 --> 00:19:59.820
where she has to fill
in her name, date.

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:02.120
Those are automatically
recognized and marked.

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:05.790
And not only that, you can see
in the upper left-hand corner,

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:07.890
since are two signers
on this document,

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:10.260
Lois, if she scrolls
down, it's also

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:12.330
recognized her fields
as well and recognized

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:13.639
which ones are hers.

00:20:13.639 --> 00:20:15.180
So all of this has
been automatically

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.940
done for her because of that
strong template integration

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:22.230
that we can do with
Google technology.

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:24.570
At this point,
Lois can go and she

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:26.502
can add an additional
initial fields

00:20:26.502 --> 00:20:27.960
on some of these
supplemental forms

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:30.300
and make sure that
Olivia is going

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:32.980
to read these documents
before she goes through that.

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:36.170
At that point she's done.

00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:39.030
Olivia, sorry, Lois can go and
she can sign her own document

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:39.530
first.

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:41.450
This is a sign and
countersign example.

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:43.610
She'll click right
here, her signature

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:46.460
comes in, she can fill in
these additional fields.

00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:48.520
And that's the magic
behind the curtain.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:50.270
I think from this
point, everybody here

00:20:50.270 --> 00:20:53.050
that said they signed a DocuSign
document before, they're

00:20:53.050 --> 00:20:54.590
used to this experience.

00:20:54.590 --> 00:20:57.770
And you can see, as most of
you know, Olivia will come in,

00:20:57.770 --> 00:20:58.490
she'll agree--

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:00.448
we're actually doing
something very interesting

00:21:00.448 --> 00:21:03.330
on the legal side to
protect your business,

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:04.880
making sure that
people acknowledge

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:07.722
the electronic signature
records and signature

00:21:07.722 --> 00:21:09.680
disclosure, which makes
us the legal equivalent

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:11.420
of a written signature.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:13.730
And we make this really
intuitive guided process.

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:16.131
If you click on that
Start button on the left,

00:21:16.131 --> 00:21:17.630
Olivia, when she
clicks that, she'll

00:21:17.630 --> 00:21:19.970
be guided right to the point
where she needs to sign.

00:21:19.970 --> 00:21:21.770
That's automatically recognized.

00:21:21.770 --> 00:21:22.850
Those are all filled out.

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:25.016
She'll initial those and
she'll finish the document.

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:29.890
At this point, this is
a lot of the real magic

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:33.470
of making all this
process digital comes in

00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:37.330
because on Lois's side she's
received an email notification

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:39.790
that that has been completed,
and that Olivia has

00:21:39.790 --> 00:21:41.119
come and signed that document.

00:21:41.119 --> 00:21:42.910
She can go and she can
look at the document

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:46.832
directly from that email,
see all this information.

00:21:46.832 --> 00:21:49.290
And what's really interesting
on her side, what you may not

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:52.284
have known is very
important for this,

00:21:52.284 --> 00:21:54.450
she can actually go and
look and see everything that

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:55.780
happened in this transaction.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:58.532
This is not a scribble
on a piece of paper.

00:21:58.532 --> 00:22:00.990
You can see every single action
by every single person that

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:03.309
was taken on this document,
when it was viewed,

00:22:03.309 --> 00:22:05.850
when it was sent, when it was
signed, did somebody look at it

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:06.990
and sign it later?

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:09.240
And you have a time stamp,
and you have an IP address,

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:10.950
and a Geo IP if it's
on a mobile device,

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:12.360
for everything that happened.

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:14.818
So you have an incredibly strong
legal protection for this.

00:22:14.818 --> 00:22:18.220
This is something that you
want for your business.

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:19.849
It's also something
very interesting.

00:22:19.849 --> 00:22:21.390
I think it changes
things culturally.

00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:24.300
In sales organizations like
Lois, some of our customers

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:26.070
tell us there is a new term.

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:28.320
Since they get
rich notifications

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:31.380
of when different things happen
in the process, at the end

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:33.180
of the quarter some
of our sales teams,

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:36.030
they tell us there's a new
phenomenon called Docu stalking

00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:38.460
where they will actually
go inside of DocuSign

00:22:38.460 --> 00:22:40.650
and hit refresh over,
and over, and over again.

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:41.640
Has my deal closed?

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:43.140
Have they signed?

00:22:43.140 --> 00:22:44.730
No more actually
taking a document,

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:47.490
printing it, putting it in a
FedEx envelope, and praying.

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:48.582
That's the old school.

00:22:48.582 --> 00:22:50.040
Well, now we've
moved to the Cloud.

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:52.470
Now that we've gone digital,
we've done that with G Suite,

00:22:52.470 --> 00:22:53.910
it suddenly becomes
entirely digital

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:56.118
and something that becomes
a rich digital transaction

00:22:56.118 --> 00:22:56.980
for you.

00:22:56.980 --> 00:22:59.280
And that's what we're
really trying to do.

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:01.050
We talked about
the days or weeks

00:23:01.050 --> 00:23:03.480
that it might take for a
FedEx envelope to get sent out

00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:05.670
and come back to you.

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:07.260
No more of that.

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:08.490
No more days, no more weeks.

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:10.720
Actually, 84% of
our transactions,

00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:14.100
since were on the Cloud, we know
are signed in less than a day.

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:17.310
Actually, about half of them
are signed in less than an hour.

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:20.040
And take all those costs that
go into, costs of lost business,

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:22.440
costs of sending,
signing, printing,

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:24.420
we actually save
about $36 per document

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:27.510
for our customers for every
single document that they sign.

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:30.300
And in no small part
thanks to the reach

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:32.610
that we get with G Suite
and the Google ecosystem,

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:35.640
we're bringing 150,000
new customers, new users,

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:37.230
into our ecosystem
every single day.

00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:44.530
And I talked about us wanting
to make every single approval,

00:23:44.530 --> 00:23:46.420
agreement,
decision-making process

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:48.550
digital for our customers.

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:50.121
We want to do that
in all of our,

00:23:50.121 --> 00:23:51.870
everybody's favorite
G Suite applications.

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:53.627
So we have
integrations with Docs,

00:23:53.627 --> 00:23:55.210
which email, as Allen
mentioned, we'll

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:59.080
be having a Docs ad-on coming
in at the end of this month.

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:02.680
And we have an
Android app as well.

00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:05.704
I think sometime around
a year and a half ago we

00:24:05.704 --> 00:24:07.870
started seeing more people
signing on mobile devices

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:10.600
than we saw signing
from a desktop

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:12.430
or from a standard web browser.

00:24:12.430 --> 00:24:15.080
So our Android has been
a huge part of that.

00:24:15.080 --> 00:24:18.970
In our Android application
we can sign Sheets,

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:20.350
we can sign Docs.

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:22.600
And all new for this
year, we recently

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:25.780
refreshed our entire design to
fit with Google material design

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:26.940
standards.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:29.560
So very interested to
help people like Louis,

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:32.620
very interested to help
all of you out there,

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:34.480
and very excited about
this new template

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:37.191
integration and this
partnership with Google.

00:24:37.191 --> 00:24:37.690
Thank you.

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:38.189
Allen?

00:24:38.189 --> 00:24:41.906
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:41.906 --> 00:24:43.710
ALLEN YANG: Thanks, Marshall.

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:46.170
We're really excited about
the new DocuSign add-on

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:48.240
and about all the other
add-ons that can soon be

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:51.100
used in Template integrations.

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:52.950
So if you're a
developer out there

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:55.920
who's interested in creating a
Template integration with us,

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:57.310
